    "q-97967": {
      "dom_id": "step-q97967",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "If $B$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\text{rank}(B-2I)=3,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\text{nullity}(B-2I)=1$\n\nII. $B-2I$ is non-singular\n\nIII. $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $B$",
      "worked_solution": "Given a $4\\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=4=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $4$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=4.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(B-2I) & =4-\\text{rank}(B-2I) \\\\  & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements II is false. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(B-2I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $B-2I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=B-2I$ is singular, then $(B-2I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $B.$ As a result, $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
