    "q-97971": {
      "dom_id": "step-q97971",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "If $B$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\text{rank}(B+3I)=2,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\text{Null}(B+3I)=\\{0\\}$\n\nII. $B+3I$ is singular\n\nIII. $\\lambda =-3$ is an eigenvalue of $B$",
      "worked_solution": "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $2$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=2.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(B+3I) & =2-\\text{rank}(B+3I) \\\\  & =2-2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\text{Null}(B+3I)=\\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statements II is false. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(B+3I)=0,$ we obtain that $B+3I$ must be non-singular.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that if $M=B+3I$ is non-singular, then $(B+3I)x=0$ has only zero solution. As a result, $\\lambda =-3$ can not be an eigenvalue of $B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
